.TH ASUS\-UX8406\-TCC\-GUARD 8 "2026" "asus\-zenbook\-duo\-ux8406" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
asus\-ux8406\-tcc\-guard \- CPU thermal\-offset guard for the ASUS Zenbook Duo UX8406
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B asus\-ux8406\-tcc\-guard
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RB [ run | restore ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The embedded controller (EC) of the ASUS Zenbook Duo UX8406 raises a thermal
warning when the lid is closed and the CPU is under load. If the CPU stays hot,
the EC cuts the power.
.PP
.B asus\-ux8406\-tcc\-guard
reads the kernel log for that warning. On a warning it raises the thermal
offset of the CPU until the temperature is below the level at which the EC
stops its alert. It then lowers the offset in steps while no new warning comes,
until the offset has its original value.
.PP
On a machine whose product name does not contain the value of the setting
.BR model ,
the program logs one line and ends with status 0.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B run
Watch for the warning and control the offset. This is the default.
.TP
.B restore
Write back the original offset that a killed guard left raised. The systemd
unit runs this after each stop.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
Configuration file, in place of
.IR /etc/asus\-ux8406\-tcc\-guard.toml .
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-\-hold\-temp " " \fIDEGREES\fR
The setting
.BR hold_temp .
.TP
.BR \-P ", " \-\-probe\-interval " " \fISECONDS\fR
The setting
.BR probe_interval .
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-time " " \fISECONDS\fR
Stop after this time. 0, the default, runs until the program is stopped.
.TP
.BR \-\-root " " \fIDIR\fR
Operate on the system below this directory. For tests.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the help.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/asus\-ux8406\-tcc\-guard.toml
Optional settings. Root must own the file, and no other user may write it. The
example file in the documentation directory of the package gives each setting
and its default.
.TP
.I /run/asus\-ux8406\-tcc\-guard
The original offset of each processor package, while an offset is raised.
.TP
.I /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:00:04.0/tcc_offset_degree_celsius
The thermal offset that the program writes.
.SH SIGNALS
On SIGTERM, SIGINT and SIGHUP the program writes the original offset back and
ends.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1)
.PP
